---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import { Counter } from "../components/Counter";

/**
 * A React component with no `client:*` directive.
 *
 * Astro renders it to HTML at build time and then throws the component away.
 * The markup is in the page; the JavaScript is not anywhere. The buttons below
 * are real buttons that do nothing, which is the honest consequence and the
 * reason the directive exists.
 */
---

<Base title="Zero JavaScript">
  <main>
    <h1>Zero JavaScript</h1>

    <p>
      The counter below is a React component, rendered at build time. There is no
      <code>client:*</code> directive on it, so Astro rendered it to HTML and shipped none of its
      code. The buttons are inert.
    </p>

    <Counter label="Static counter" testId="static-counter" />

    <p class="note">
      View source: the markup is there. Check the network tab: there is no JavaScript at all. The
      e2e suite asserts exactly that, by counting script responses.
    </p>

    <h2>Why this is the default</h2>
    <p>
      Most of a content page is not interactive. A React SPA sends the framework, the router and
      every component regardless, because it has no way to know which parts a user will touch.
      Astro's answer is to make you say.
    </p>
  </main>
</Base>
